1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,980
Have you ever noticed that some people genuinely enjoy being alone?

2
00:00:04,520 --> 00:00:06,180
They don't need constant conversations,

3
00:00:06,820 --> 00:00:09,060
they don't feel the urge to attend every gathering,

4
00:00:09,640 --> 00:00:13,300
and they can spend an entire evening by themselves without feeling like

5
00:00:13,300 --> 00:00:14,140
something is missing.

6
00:00:14,680 --> 00:00:16,620
To other people, this might seem strange.

7
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:20,580
Why would anyone choose an empty room over a room full of

8
00:00:20,580 --> 00:00:20,820
friends?

9
00:00:21,420 --> 00:00:24,240
Why turn down invitations when you could be making memories?

10
00:00:24,920 --> 00:00:28,280
And why do some people feel more comfortable with their own thoughts

11
00:00:28,280 --> 00:00:29,240
than with a crowd

12
00:00:29,240 --> 00:00:29,800
of people?

13
00:00:30,260 --> 00:00:32,800
We often assume that people who spend a lot of time alone

14
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:33,780
are lonely,

15
00:00:34,380 --> 00:00:35,360
socially awkward,

16
00:00:35,580 --> 00:00:37,280
or unable to connect with others.

17
00:00:37,920 --> 00:00:39,980
But what if we're misunderstanding them?

18
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:43,540
What if for some people being alone isn't an escape from life?

19
00:00:44,260 --> 00:00:47,260
What if it's the place where they finally feel most connected to

20
00:00:47,260 --> 00:00:47,440
it?

21
00:00:48,000 --> 00:00:51,600
Today, we're exploring the psychology of people who enjoy solitude,

22
00:00:52,040 --> 00:00:53,140
why some intelligent

23
00:00:53,140 --> 00:00:55,040
people prefer spending time alone,

24
00:00:55,200 --> 00:00:58,100
and what the difference between being alone and feeling

25
00:00:58,100 --> 00:01:00,240
lonely reveals about the human mind.

26
00:01:00,740 --> 00:01:02,160
Welcome to Why We Become.

27
00:01:02,660 --> 00:01:04,140
Let's start with something important.

28
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:07,980
Being alone and being lonely are not the same thing.

29
00:01:08,400 --> 00:01:11,660
You can be surrounded by dozens of people and still feel completely

30
00:01:11,660 --> 00:01:12,300
misunderstood.

31
00:01:13,220 --> 00:01:14,320
You can laugh with friends,

32
00:01:14,560 --> 00:01:15,680
participate in conversations,

33
00:01:16,280 --> 00:01:17,660
and appear perfectly happy

34
00:01:17,660 --> 00:01:19,840
while feeling like nobody really knows you.

35
00:01:20,220 --> 00:01:23,540
And you can sit alone in a quiet room with nobody around

36
00:01:23,540 --> 00:01:25,040
you and feel completely at

37
00:01:25,040 --> 00:01:25,340
peace.

38
00:01:25,340 --> 00:01:28,740
The difference isn't simply how many people are present.

39
00:01:29,320 --> 00:01:32,980
It's how you experience your own company and your connection with others.

40
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:37,140
Loneliness is the feeling that the connection you need is missing.

41
00:01:37,880 --> 00:01:40,000
Solitude is the experience of being alone.

42
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:44,060
Sometimes solitude feels peaceful, sometimes loneliness hurts,

43
00:01:44,480 --> 00:01:45,880
and sometimes the two exist

44
00:01:45,880 --> 00:01:46,300
together.

45
00:01:46,740 --> 00:01:49,940
A person can enjoy spending time alone and still need love,

46
00:01:50,200 --> 00:01:51,340
friendship, and meaningful

47
00:01:51,340 --> 00:01:52,000
relationships.

48
00:01:52,000 --> 00:01:56,920
They can appreciate silence without wanting to be isolated from the world.

49
00:01:57,860 --> 00:02:01,620
Understanding this distinction changes how we look at people who prefer their

50
00:02:01,620 --> 00:02:02,240
own company,

51
00:02:02,620 --> 00:02:05,680
because instead of asking why they don't want to be around people

52
00:02:05,680 --> 00:02:06,280
all the time,

53
00:02:06,600 --> 00:02:08,360
we can ask a more interesting question.

54
00:02:08,940 --> 00:02:13,040
What do they experience when they're alone that they struggle to find

55
00:02:13,040 --> 00:02:13,660
elsewhere?

56
00:02:14,120 --> 00:02:17,460
For some people, the answer begins with how they process the world

57
00:02:17,460 --> 00:02:17,940
around them.

58
00:02:18,500 --> 00:02:20,200
Think about a typical social gathering.

59
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:22,620
There's music playing in the background.

60
00:02:23,180 --> 00:02:25,000
Several conversations are happening at once.

61
00:02:25,580 --> 00:02:27,580
People are laughing, telling stories,

62
00:02:27,860 --> 00:02:30,520
reacting to jokes, and moving from one conversation

63
00:02:30,520 --> 00:02:31,200
to another.

64
00:02:31,740 --> 00:02:34,120
Some people feel energized by this environment.

65
00:02:34,600 --> 00:02:35,900
They enjoy the stimulation.

66
00:02:36,440 --> 00:02:38,860
They feel more alive when they're interacting with others.

67
00:02:39,380 --> 00:02:42,260
But other people experience the same environment differently.

68
00:02:42,900 --> 00:02:44,200
They might enjoy the conversations,

69
00:02:44,460 --> 00:02:45,640
but after a few hours,

70
00:02:45,640 --> 00:02:47,220
they feel mentally exhausted.

71
00:02:47,920 --> 00:02:48,640
They need quiet.

72
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:50,280
They want to go home,

73
00:02:50,560 --> 00:02:54,420
put their phone away, and spend some time without having to respond

74
00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:55,040
to anyone.

75
00:02:55,520 --> 00:02:57,940
This is often associated with introversion.

76
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:01,040
Introversion doesn't necessarily mean being shy,

77
00:03:01,380 --> 00:03:03,380
antisocial, or afraid of people.

78
00:03:03,960 --> 00:03:05,980
An introverted person can be confident,

79
00:03:06,500 --> 00:03:07,280
socially skilled,

80
00:03:07,800 --> 00:03:09,640
funny, and comfortable speaking in

81
00:03:09,640 --> 00:03:10,180
front of a crowd.

82
00:03:10,820 --> 00:03:14,800
The difference is that certain kinds of social interaction may require more

83
00:03:14,800 --> 00:03:15,780
energy from them,

84
00:03:15,780 --> 00:03:18,480
while solitude gives them an opportunity to recharge.

85
00:03:19,200 --> 00:03:23,520
Of course, people are more complicated than a simple introvert or extrovert

86
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:23,820
label.

87
00:03:24,440 --> 00:03:24,980
Your personality,

88
00:03:25,560 --> 00:03:26,040
environment,

89
00:03:26,580 --> 00:03:26,800
mood,

90
00:03:27,800 --> 00:03:31,040
relationships, and circumstances all influence how much social

91
00:03:31,040 --> 00:03:31,960
interaction you want.

92
00:03:32,540 --> 00:03:34,220
But the broader point is important.

93
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:38,140
Not everyone experiences the same environment in the same way.

94
00:03:38,560 --> 00:03:41,080
For one person, a busy room feels exciting.

95
00:03:41,460 --> 00:03:43,160
For another, it feels overwhelming.

96
00:03:43,160 --> 00:03:46,820
Neither reaction automatically makes one person better,

97
00:03:47,420 --> 00:03:49,520
smarter, or more emotionally mature

98
00:03:49,520 --> 00:03:50,140
than the other.

99
00:03:50,540 --> 00:03:52,000
They simply have different needs.

100
00:03:52,660 --> 00:03:56,760
And problems begin when we assume that everyone should enjoy the same

101
00:03:56,760 --> 00:03:58,000
amount of social activity.

102
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:00,880
If someone constantly needs time alone,

103
00:04:01,320 --> 00:04:03,360
people may interpret it as rejection.

104
00:04:03,940 --> 00:04:07,240
They might think, why doesn't this person want to spend time with

105
00:04:07,240 --> 00:04:07,460
us?

106
00:04:07,900 --> 00:04:10,420
But sometimes the person isn't rejecting anyone.

107
00:04:10,420 --> 00:04:13,920
They're simply recognizing what they need to feel balanced.

108
00:04:14,640 --> 00:04:18,060
And learning to respect that need can make their relationships healthier,

109
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:18,800
not weaker.

110
00:04:19,440 --> 00:04:21,120
Now let's talk about intelligence.

111
00:04:21,980 --> 00:04:25,760
You've probably heard the idea that intelligent people prefer to be alone.

112
00:04:26,220 --> 00:04:29,020
There's a reason this idea attracts so much attention.

113
00:04:29,420 --> 00:04:32,820
It suggests that people who feel different from the crowd might have

114
00:04:32,820 --> 00:04:33,940
a special explanation

115
00:04:33,940 --> 00:04:35,680
for why they don't always fit in.

116
00:04:35,680 --> 00:04:40,280
But the relationship between intelligence and solitude is more complicated than that.

117
00:04:40,860 --> 00:04:43,400
Research has explored connections between intelligence,

118
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:44,240
personality,

119
00:04:44,780 --> 00:04:45,560
social interaction,

120
00:04:45,920 --> 00:04:46,780
and life satisfaction.

121
00:04:47,440 --> 00:04:52,120
Some findings suggest that the relationship between socializing and happiness can differ

122
00:04:52,120 --> 00:04:54,840
depending on a person's characteristics and circumstances.

123
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:58,960
But there is no universal rule that intelligent people prefer to be

124
00:04:58,960 --> 00:04:59,280
alone.

125
00:04:59,760 --> 00:05:02,700
Being introverted doesn't prove that someone is intelligent.

126
00:05:02,700 --> 00:05:07,080
And enjoying solitude doesn't automatically make someone more insightful than the people

127
00:05:07,080 --> 00:05:07,620
around them.

128
00:05:08,040 --> 00:05:10,080
So why do some thoughtful,

129
00:05:10,460 --> 00:05:14,240
intellectually curious people genuinely enjoy spending time alone?

130
00:05:14,860 --> 00:05:18,600
One possible explanation is that solitude gives them the space to engage

131
00:05:18,600 --> 00:05:19,360
deeply with

132
00:05:19,360 --> 00:05:20,020
their own interests.

133
00:05:20,600 --> 00:05:22,540
Think about someone learning a difficult skill.

134
00:05:23,020 --> 00:05:25,480
Maybe they're studying philosophy, writing a book,

135
00:05:25,760 --> 00:05:26,460
building a business,

136
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:30,200
learning a language, or trying to understand a complicated idea.

137
00:05:30,200 --> 00:05:32,460
Deep work requires attention.

138
00:05:33,100 --> 00:05:36,400
It requires staying with a problem long enough to move beyond the

139
00:05:36,400 --> 00:05:37,220
obvious answer.

140
00:05:37,800 --> 00:05:40,620
And that can be difficult when your attention is constantly being pulled

141
00:05:40,620 --> 00:05:41,500
in different directions.

142
00:05:42,120 --> 00:05:43,220
A notification appears.

143
00:05:43,760 --> 00:05:44,980
Someone asks a question.

144
00:05:45,420 --> 00:05:46,440
A conversation begins.

145
00:05:47,080 --> 00:05:49,900
You check your phone and suddenly 20 minutes have disappeared.

146
00:05:50,540 --> 00:05:52,820
None of these things is necessarily bad.

147
00:05:53,460 --> 00:05:54,580
Human connection matters.

148
00:05:55,320 --> 00:05:58,760
Conversations can introduce us to ideas we would never discover alone.

149
00:05:58,760 --> 00:06:02,980
But certain kinds of thinking need uninterrupted time.

150
00:06:03,560 --> 00:06:06,320
When you're alone, you can follow a question wherever it leads.

151
00:06:06,880 --> 00:06:10,020
You can read something confusing twice without feeling embarrassed.

152
00:06:10,700 --> 00:06:13,740
You can change your mind without having to defend yourself.

153
00:06:14,300 --> 00:06:17,760
You can spend an hour exploring an idea that nobody else around

154
00:06:17,760 --> 00:06:18,740
you finds interesting.

155
00:06:19,180 --> 00:06:22,880
And you don't have to turn every thought into something entertaining for

156
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:23,540
another person.

157
00:06:23,540 --> 00:06:26,220
For people who enjoy intellectual exploration,

158
00:06:26,680 --> 00:06:28,540
this freedom can be deeply satisfying.

159
00:06:29,120 --> 00:06:32,240
Their minds aren't necessarily more active than everyone else's.

160
00:06:32,540 --> 00:06:36,080
They simply enjoy having the space to use their attention in a

161
00:06:36,080 --> 00:06:37,680
way that feels meaningful to them.

162
00:06:38,020 --> 00:06:42,440
And sometimes the most valuable thing solitude offers isn't an extraordinary discovery.

163
00:06:43,020 --> 00:06:45,320
It's the ability to think without interruption.

164
00:06:45,780 --> 00:06:49,340
There's another reason some people become more comfortable spending time alone.

165
00:06:49,340 --> 00:06:53,400
They learn to enjoy activities without needing external validation.

166
00:06:54,240 --> 00:06:55,280
Imagine reading a book you love.

167
00:06:55,820 --> 00:06:57,580
You don't need anyone to see you reading it.

168
00:06:57,960 --> 00:06:59,620
You don't need to post a photograph of it.

169
00:06:59,900 --> 00:07:02,640
You don't need someone to tell you that reading makes you productive

170
00:07:02,640 --> 00:07:03,640
or intelligent.

171
00:07:04,200 --> 00:07:05,980
You simply enjoy the experience.

172
00:07:06,520 --> 00:07:08,500
The same can happen when you're walking through nature,

173
00:07:09,020 --> 00:07:11,320
learning a skill, exercising, drawing,

174
00:07:11,660 --> 00:07:13,760
cooking, or sitting quietly with your thoughts.

175
00:07:14,280 --> 00:07:17,660
The activity has value even when nobody else knows you're doing it.

176
00:07:17,660 --> 00:07:19,440
This might seem like a small thing,

177
00:07:19,780 --> 00:07:21,560
but it reflects an important shift.

178
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:25,720
You stop treating every experience as something that needs to be witnessed,

179
00:07:26,280 --> 00:07:28,540
approved, or validated by other people.

180
00:07:29,020 --> 00:07:31,240
You begin to appreciate things for what they are,

181
00:07:31,540 --> 00:07:33,260
not just for how they make you appear.

182
00:07:33,720 --> 00:07:35,300
And when you develop that ability,

183
00:07:35,680 --> 00:07:37,980
spending time alone can become much more rewarding.

184
00:07:38,440 --> 00:07:41,480
You no longer need constant feedback to feel that your time has

185
00:07:41,480 --> 00:07:41,840
meaning.

186
00:07:42,400 --> 00:07:46,020
You can enjoy an ordinary afternoon without turning it into a performance.

187
00:07:46,620 --> 00:07:49,400
You can make progress on a goal without announcing it.

188
00:07:49,880 --> 00:07:52,940
You can have an experience that belongs entirely to you.

189
00:07:53,400 --> 00:07:55,620
That doesn't mean you should stop sharing your life with others.

190
00:07:56,440 --> 00:07:58,540
Sharing experiences can make them more meaningful.

191
00:07:59,160 --> 00:08:02,080
Celebrating achievements with people you care about can be wonderful.

192
00:08:02,680 --> 00:08:05,700
The point is simply that your happiness doesn't have to depend on

193
00:08:05,700 --> 00:08:06,200
someone else

194
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:07,840
noticing everything you do.

195
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:11,560
Some experiences can be valuable even when they remain private.

196
00:08:11,560 --> 00:08:14,120
And the more comfortable you become with that idea,

197
00:08:14,500 --> 00:08:18,120
the less pressure you may feel to fill every quiet moment with

198
00:08:18,120 --> 00:08:19,420
external stimulation.

199
00:08:19,940 --> 00:08:23,280
But solitude offers something else that is easy to overlook.

200
00:08:23,820 --> 00:08:26,480
It gives you space to hear your own thoughts.

201
00:08:27,040 --> 00:08:30,000
Think about how often your day is shaped by other people's expectations.

202
00:08:30,780 --> 00:08:32,800
You hear what your family thinks you should do.

203
00:08:33,200 --> 00:08:34,680
You see what your friends are achieving.

204
00:08:35,220 --> 00:08:38,340
You scroll through social media and discover what people your age are

205
00:08:38,340 --> 00:08:38,840
buying,

206
00:08:38,840 --> 00:08:41,960
where they're traveling, and how successful they appear to be.

207
00:08:42,400 --> 00:08:45,200
You listen to opinions about what a good life should look like.

208
00:08:45,620 --> 00:08:48,400
And slowly, without necessarily noticing it,

209
00:08:48,720 --> 00:08:51,820
those expectations can become mixed up with your own desires.

210
00:08:52,340 --> 00:08:55,660
You start wanting things because everyone around you seems to want them.

211
00:08:56,040 --> 00:08:58,740
You judge your progress according to other people's timelines.

212
00:08:59,420 --> 00:09:01,560
You begin to wonder whether your choices are right,

213
00:09:01,920 --> 00:09:04,380
simply because they look different from everyone else's.

214
00:09:04,660 --> 00:09:07,360
And when you're constantly surrounded by other people's opinions,

215
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:10,180
it can become difficult to identify your own.

216
00:09:10,900 --> 00:09:13,980
Solitude creates an opportunity to step away from that noise.

217
00:09:14,500 --> 00:09:18,000
When you're alone, you can ask yourself questions you might otherwise avoid.

218
00:09:18,540 --> 00:09:20,280
Do I actually want this career?

219
00:09:20,580 --> 00:09:23,040
Or do I want the respect that comes with it?

220
00:09:23,480 --> 00:09:26,060
Am I pursuing this goal because it matters to me?

221
00:09:26,200 --> 00:09:28,260
Or because I don't want to feel left behind?

222
00:09:28,960 --> 00:09:31,400
Do I genuinely enjoy the way I'm living?

223
00:09:31,740 --> 00:09:34,040
Or have I simply become accustomed to it?

224
00:09:34,040 --> 00:09:37,220
These questions don't always produce comfortable answers.

225
00:09:37,860 --> 00:09:41,340
Sometimes, they reveal that you've been following a path you never consciously

226
00:09:41,340 --> 00:09:41,780
chose.

227
00:09:42,580 --> 00:09:45,920
Sometimes, they force you to acknowledge that you're unhappy with something you've

228
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:46,020
been

229
00:09:46,020 --> 00:09:47,200
pretending is fine.

230
00:09:47,820 --> 00:09:51,380
And sometimes, they help you realize that the life you genuinely want

231
00:09:51,380 --> 00:09:54,600
looks very different from the life you've been trying to impress other

232
00:09:54,600 --> 00:09:55,220
people with.

233
00:09:55,720 --> 00:09:59,180
But you cannot always hear those answers when your attention is constantly

234
00:09:59,180 --> 00:09:59,800
occupied.

235
00:09:59,800 --> 00:10:03,860
You need moments when you aren't reacting to someone else's priorities.

236
00:10:04,640 --> 00:10:06,240
Moments when you can examine your own.

237
00:10:06,740 --> 00:10:09,340
That's one of the most useful things solitude can provide.

238
00:10:09,860 --> 00:10:12,320
Not because being alone automatically makes you wiser,

239
00:10:13,020 --> 00:10:15,340
but because it gives you an opportunity to reflect,

240
00:10:15,920 --> 00:10:18,840
question your assumptions, and decide what matters to you.

241
00:10:19,320 --> 00:10:19,620
Of course,

242
00:10:20,180 --> 00:10:23,260
reflection only becomes useful when it helps you understand your life more

243
00:10:23,260 --> 00:10:23,720
clearly.

244
00:10:24,120 --> 00:10:27,480
If you spend hours thinking about the same problems without reaching new

245
00:10:27,480 --> 00:10:27,920
insights

246
00:10:27,920 --> 00:10:28,920
or taking action,

247
00:10:29,780 --> 00:10:31,100
solitude can turn into rumination.

248
00:10:31,680 --> 00:10:34,360
And rumination can leave you feeling worse rather than better.

249
00:10:34,800 --> 00:10:36,480
The goal isn't to think endlessly.

250
00:10:37,100 --> 00:10:40,460
It's to create enough quiet to understand what you're thinking,

251
00:10:40,940 --> 00:10:43,520
then use that understanding to live more intentionally.

252
00:10:44,220 --> 00:10:47,800
Now, there's another important reason people sometimes prefer being alone.

253
00:10:48,600 --> 00:10:52,160
Social interaction can require a surprising amount of emotional work.

254
00:10:52,700 --> 00:10:56,280
Imagine spending the entire day trying to meet other people's expectations.

255
00:10:56,280 --> 00:10:59,400
You adjust your tone depending on who you're speaking to.

256
00:10:59,820 --> 00:11:02,140
You pay attention to how your words might be interpreted.

257
00:11:02,680 --> 00:11:04,580
You try to avoid making anyone uncomfortable.

258
00:11:05,180 --> 00:11:08,080
You listen to problems, respond to emotions,

259
00:11:08,520 --> 00:11:11,380
and decide how much of your own life you're comfortable sharing.

260
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:15,760
In some situations, this is simply part of healthy communication.

261
00:11:16,480 --> 00:11:18,400
Relationships require consideration,

262
00:11:18,920 --> 00:11:19,180
empathy,

263
00:11:19,740 --> 00:11:20,540
and compromise.

264
00:11:21,060 --> 00:11:24,560
But if you constantly feel that you have to perform a version

265
00:11:24,560 --> 00:11:26,080
of yourself to be accepted,

266
00:11:26,760 --> 00:11:28,960
social interaction can become exhausting.

267
00:11:29,640 --> 00:11:33,120
Maybe you hide your interests because you're worried people will judge them.

268
00:11:33,580 --> 00:11:36,740
Maybe you agree with opinions you don't share because you don't want

269
00:11:36,740 --> 00:11:37,900
to create conflict.

270
00:11:38,500 --> 00:11:40,460
Maybe you feel pressure to be entertaining,

271
00:11:41,080 --> 00:11:41,280
successful,

272
00:11:42,120 --> 00:11:43,900
confident, or constantly available.

273
00:11:44,580 --> 00:11:49,140
Over time, you might begin associating social situations with the effort of

274
00:11:49,140 --> 00:11:50,060
managing other

275
00:11:50,060 --> 00:11:50,960
people's impressions.

276
00:11:51,740 --> 00:11:54,000
And being alone starts to feel like relief.

277
00:11:54,520 --> 00:11:56,460
You don't have to explain why you're quiet.

278
00:11:56,980 --> 00:11:59,040
You don't have to pretend to be in a good mood.

279
00:11:59,580 --> 00:12:02,120
You don't have to make every conversation interesting.

280
00:12:02,740 --> 00:12:06,380
You can simply exist without worrying about how you're being perceived.

281
00:12:06,880 --> 00:12:09,160
That freedom can be incredibly valuable.

282
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:10,760
But here's the distinction.

283
00:12:11,360 --> 00:12:14,100
Enjoying the freedom to be yourself is healthy.

284
00:12:14,640 --> 00:12:19,080
Avoiding every situation where you might be misunderstood is something different.

285
00:12:19,080 --> 00:12:22,600
If you withdraw because you genuinely enjoy solitude,

286
00:12:23,120 --> 00:12:23,780
that's one thing.

287
00:12:24,260 --> 00:12:26,880
If you withdraw because you're terrified of rejection,

288
00:12:27,440 --> 00:12:28,940
criticism, or embarrassment,

289
00:12:29,660 --> 00:12:32,940
the experience may be driven more by fear than by preference.

290
00:12:33,480 --> 00:12:36,640
From the outside, both people might look exactly the same.

291
00:12:37,100 --> 00:12:38,060
They spend time alone.

292
00:12:38,560 --> 00:12:39,600
They decline invitations.

293
00:12:40,020 --> 00:12:41,800
They keep their social circles small.

294
00:12:42,420 --> 00:12:45,560
But internally, their experiences can be very different.

295
00:12:46,020 --> 00:12:47,760
One feels peaceful and fulfilled.

296
00:12:47,760 --> 00:12:51,660
The other wants connection but doesn't know how to approach it without

297
00:12:51,660 --> 00:12:52,520
feeling anxious.

298
00:12:53,160 --> 00:12:56,380
This is why we shouldn't automatically romanticize isolation.

299
00:12:56,960 --> 00:13:00,800
Not every person who stays alone is secretly more intelligent,

300
00:13:01,260 --> 00:13:04,300
more mature, or more independent than everyone else.

301
00:13:04,860 --> 00:13:06,560
Sometimes people need solitude.

302
00:13:07,200 --> 00:13:08,900
Sometimes they need support.

303
00:13:09,380 --> 00:13:12,480
And sometimes they need to learn how to build relationships

304
00:13:12,480 --> 00:13:14,840
that feel safe enough for them to be themselves.

305
00:13:14,840 --> 00:13:18,980
The important question isn't simply whether you spend time alone.

306
00:13:19,620 --> 00:13:21,960
It's whether that time helps you live a fuller life.

307
00:13:22,720 --> 00:13:27,360
Consider the difference between choosing solitude and using solitude to hide.

308
00:13:27,880 --> 00:13:31,020
When you choose solitude, you can enjoy your own company

309
00:13:31,020 --> 00:13:33,620
while remaining open to meaningful relationships.

310
00:13:34,220 --> 00:13:37,300
You can spend a weekend alone and still look forward to meeting

311
00:13:37,300 --> 00:13:38,320
a friend next week.

312
00:13:38,800 --> 00:13:41,520
You can turn down an invitation because you need rest,

313
00:13:41,520 --> 00:13:44,740
not because you're convinced that nobody will understand you.

314
00:13:45,140 --> 00:13:48,780
You can enjoy your independence without treating every relationship as a threat

315
00:13:48,780 --> 00:13:49,360
to it.

316
00:13:49,580 --> 00:13:52,400
But when solitude becomes a way to avoid life,

317
00:13:52,880 --> 00:13:54,360
the pattern starts to look different.

318
00:13:54,720 --> 00:13:57,440
You stop reaching out, even when you want connection.

319
00:13:57,880 --> 00:14:00,700
You avoid new experiences because they involve uncertainty.

320
00:14:01,340 --> 00:14:03,600
You convince yourself that you don't need anyone,

321
00:14:04,020 --> 00:14:05,280
even though you feel isolated.

322
00:14:05,820 --> 00:14:09,800
You begin interpreting every disagreement as proof that people are not worth

323
00:14:09,800 --> 00:14:10,280
the effort.

324
00:14:10,280 --> 00:14:11,240
And slowly,

325
00:14:11,860 --> 00:14:12,860
the world becomes smaller.

326
00:14:13,220 --> 00:14:16,400
At first, avoiding uncomfortable situations may bring relief.

327
00:14:16,920 --> 00:14:18,880
But that relief can reinforce the avoidance.

328
00:14:19,480 --> 00:14:20,840
The less you interact with people,

329
00:14:21,160 --> 00:14:24,060
the more unfamiliar social situations may begin to feel.

330
00:14:24,600 --> 00:14:25,980
And the more unfamiliar they feel,

331
00:14:26,300 --> 00:14:28,200
the easier it becomes to avoid them again.

332
00:14:28,720 --> 00:14:32,580
Eventually, what started as a preference for quiet can become a habit

333
00:14:32,580 --> 00:14:33,160
of withdrawal.

334
00:14:33,660 --> 00:14:36,060
This doesn't happen to everyone who enjoys solitude.

335
00:14:36,060 --> 00:14:39,960
And spending a lot of time alone is not automatically a warning

336
00:14:39,960 --> 00:14:40,400
sign.

337
00:14:40,900 --> 00:14:44,080
But it's worth paying attention to how your choices affect you over

338
00:14:44,080 --> 00:14:44,440
time.

339
00:14:45,020 --> 00:14:47,600
Are you choosing solitude because it makes your life richer?

340
00:14:48,200 --> 00:14:51,300
Or are you choosing it because you've stopped believing that connection is

341
00:14:51,300 --> 00:14:51,740
possible?

342
00:14:52,220 --> 00:14:53,760
Those are very different situations.

343
00:14:54,420 --> 00:14:57,920
And recognizing the difference can help you protect both your independence

344
00:14:57,920 --> 00:15:00,320
and your ability to connect with others.

345
00:15:00,860 --> 00:15:04,400
There's also something important about the quality of your relationships.

346
00:15:04,880 --> 00:15:09,280
Some people prefer a small social circle because they value depth over

347
00:15:09,280 --> 00:15:09,800
frequency.

348
00:15:10,440 --> 00:15:12,700
They might not want to speak to dozens of people every day.

349
00:15:13,140 --> 00:15:17,400
They might not enjoy casual conversations that never move beyond familiar topics.

350
00:15:17,980 --> 00:15:18,380
Instead,

351
00:15:18,980 --> 00:15:21,660
they value a few relationships where they can be honest,

352
00:15:22,060 --> 00:15:24,240
curious, and emotionally present.

353
00:15:24,780 --> 00:15:29,020
They want conversations where people can disagree without attacking each other.

354
00:15:29,520 --> 00:15:33,400
Where they can discuss difficult questions without pretending to have every answer.

355
00:15:33,400 --> 00:15:35,960
Where silence doesn't have to be awkward.

356
00:15:36,580 --> 00:15:39,740
Where they can share something personal without worrying that it would become

357
00:15:39,740 --> 00:15:41,360
entertainment for everyone else.

358
00:15:41,920 --> 00:15:45,680
For these people, a smaller social circle can feel more satisfying

359
00:15:45,680 --> 00:15:48,320
than a large network of casual connections.

360
00:15:48,880 --> 00:15:52,060
But again, this isn't exclusive to intelligent people.

361
00:15:52,640 --> 00:15:55,140
It's a preference that can emerge from personality,

362
00:15:55,740 --> 00:15:56,460
life experience,

363
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:58,380
values, and circumstances.

364
00:15:59,100 --> 00:16:01,540
And it doesn't mean casual friendships are worthless.

365
00:16:02,100 --> 00:16:04,120
Small interactions can create happiness,

366
00:16:04,700 --> 00:16:06,340
belonging, and a sense of community.

367
00:16:06,820 --> 00:16:08,340
A brief conversation with a neighbor,

368
00:16:08,760 --> 00:16:10,360
a friendly exchange with a colleague,

369
00:16:10,880 --> 00:16:14,040
or a shared joke with someone you barely know can make an

370
00:16:14,040 --> 00:16:15,620
ordinary day feel better.

371
00:16:16,140 --> 00:16:18,340
Not every interaction needs to be profound.

372
00:16:18,860 --> 00:16:22,380
The point is that different relationships serve different purposes.

373
00:16:23,040 --> 00:16:26,960
You might enjoy being friendly with many people while reserving your deepest

374
00:16:26,960 --> 00:16:27,380
thoughts

375
00:16:27,380 --> 00:16:28,760
for a few trusted friends.

376
00:16:28,760 --> 00:16:33,540
You might enjoy social gatherings occasionally without wanting them to dominate your

377
00:16:33,540 --> 00:16:33,800
life.

378
00:16:34,500 --> 00:16:38,120
You might prefer quiet evenings while still valuing the people who make

379
00:16:38,120 --> 00:16:39,080
you feel understood.

380
00:16:39,640 --> 00:16:43,300
You don't have to choose between being independent and being connected.

381
00:16:43,820 --> 00:16:45,700
A healthy life can contain both.

382
00:16:46,300 --> 00:16:49,560
And perhaps that's the most misunderstood part of solitude.

383
00:16:50,100 --> 00:16:54,000
People often imagine that someone who enjoys being alone must be choosing

384
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:54,980
between themselves

385
00:16:54,980 --> 00:16:56,220
and everyone else.

386
00:16:56,220 --> 00:17:01,240
But a person can enjoy their own company without rejecting the company

387
00:17:01,240 --> 00:17:01,660
of others.

388
00:17:02,340 --> 00:17:06,340
They can be comfortable alone without believing they're better off alone in

389
00:17:06,340 --> 00:17:07,060
every situation.

390
00:17:07,860 --> 00:17:12,160
They can value independence while understanding that humans still need connection.

391
00:17:12,760 --> 00:17:16,560
Because independence isn't the absence of relationships.

392
00:17:17,060 --> 00:17:21,340
It's the ability to participate in relationships without losing your sense of

393
00:17:21,340 --> 00:17:21,580
self.

394
00:17:22,120 --> 00:17:25,120
And that brings us to a final question.

395
00:17:25,720 --> 00:17:30,200
Why does being alone sometimes become more enjoyable as people grow older

396
00:17:30,200 --> 00:17:31,340
and understand

397
00:17:31,340 --> 00:17:32,220
themselves better?

398
00:17:32,760 --> 00:17:36,800
One possible reason is that self-knowledge changes what you look for in

399
00:17:36,800 --> 00:17:37,180
your time.

400
00:17:37,760 --> 00:17:39,520
When you don't understand your own preferences,

401
00:17:39,800 --> 00:17:41,360
it's easy to follow the crowd.

402
00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:44,220
You attend events because everyone else is going.

403
00:17:44,580 --> 00:17:47,280
You pursue goals because they're socially rewarded.

404
00:17:47,840 --> 00:17:51,260
You fill your schedule because being busy makes you feel important.

405
00:17:51,260 --> 00:17:53,400
But as you learn more about yourself,

406
00:17:53,760 --> 00:17:55,580
you become more selective.

407
00:17:56,300 --> 00:17:59,340
You recognize which activities genuinely energize you.

408
00:17:59,720 --> 00:18:03,920
You understand which relationships are meaningful and which ones leave you feeling

409
00:18:03,920 --> 00:18:04,260
drained.

410
00:18:04,820 --> 00:18:07,680
You become more comfortable saying no to things that don't fit your

411
00:18:07,680 --> 00:18:08,200
priorities.

412
00:18:08,840 --> 00:18:12,200
And you stop assuming that every opportunity deserves your attention.

413
00:18:12,700 --> 00:18:17,000
This doesn't mean you should avoid challenges or surround yourself only with

414
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:17,400
people who

415
00:18:17,400 --> 00:18:18,120
agree with you.

416
00:18:18,900 --> 00:18:20,660
Growth often requires discomfort.

417
00:18:20,660 --> 00:18:23,740
Sometimes you need to enter unfamiliar environments,

418
00:18:24,320 --> 00:18:26,060
listen to perspectives you disagree with,

419
00:18:26,260 --> 00:18:26,520
and

420
00:18:26,520 --> 00:18:29,960
build relationships with people whose experiences differ from yours.

421
00:18:30,660 --> 00:18:32,500
But you can do those things intentionally.

422
00:18:33,120 --> 00:18:36,580
You don't have to fill every hour with activities simply to prove

423
00:18:36,580 --> 00:18:36,960
that you're

424
00:18:36,960 --> 00:18:37,760
living a full life.

425
00:18:38,260 --> 00:18:41,480
You don't have to attend every gathering to prove that you're social.

426
00:18:41,980 --> 00:18:45,660
And you don't have to keep yourself constantly entertained to prove that

427
00:18:45,660 --> 00:18:46,100
you're happy.

428
00:18:46,620 --> 00:18:49,560
Sometimes a quiet afternoon is enough.

429
00:18:50,060 --> 00:18:52,600
Sometimes reading a book, taking a walk,

430
00:18:52,940 --> 00:18:54,460
working on a meaningful project,

431
00:18:54,720 --> 00:18:56,080
or sitting with your

432
00:18:56,080 --> 00:19:00,040
own thoughts can be more satisfying than another evening spent doing something

433
00:19:00,040 --> 00:19:00,620
you don't

434
00:19:00,620 --> 00:19:01,880
actually want to do.

435
00:19:02,280 --> 00:19:05,280
The goal isn't to become someone who never needs other people.

436
00:19:05,820 --> 00:19:09,020
The goal is to become comfortable enough with yourself that you can

437
00:19:09,020 --> 00:19:09,580
choose your

438
00:19:09,580 --> 00:19:12,500
relationships and experiences with greater intention.

439
00:19:13,180 --> 00:19:15,600
You can be alone without feeling incomplete.

440
00:19:15,600 --> 00:19:18,660
And you can be with others without feeling that you need to

441
00:19:18,660 --> 00:19:19,640
become someone else.

442
00:19:20,520 --> 00:19:21,860
So if you enjoy solitude,

443
00:19:22,240 --> 00:19:24,900
you don't need to treat that preference as a problem that

444
00:19:24,900 --> 00:19:25,660
must be fixed.

445
00:19:26,160 --> 00:19:28,760
You also don't need to turn it into proof that you're more

446
00:19:28,760 --> 00:19:30,600
intelligent or more evolved

447
00:19:30,600 --> 00:19:31,840
than everyone around you.

448
00:19:32,320 --> 00:19:35,680
You can simply recognize that quiet has value in your life.

449
00:19:36,220 --> 00:19:36,900
Use it to think.

450
00:19:37,480 --> 00:19:38,280
Use it to create.

451
00:19:38,960 --> 00:19:40,600
Use it to understand your priorities.

452
00:19:41,260 --> 00:19:43,600
Use it to rest when the world becomes overwhelming.

453
00:19:43,600 --> 00:19:47,160
And when you find people with whom you can be genuinely yourself,

454
00:19:48,020 --> 00:19:48,380
allow those

455
00:19:48,380 --> 00:19:50,560
relationships to have a place in your life too.

456
00:19:50,980 --> 00:19:54,100
Because the real measure of a fulfilling life isn't how much time

457
00:19:54,100 --> 00:19:55,280
you spend alone or

458
00:19:55,280 --> 00:19:56,800
how many people surround you.

459
00:19:57,180 --> 00:20:00,420
It's whether your life contains the right balance of independence,

460
00:20:01,400 --> 00:20:01,440
connection,

461
00:20:02,100 --> 00:20:02,140
purpose,

462
00:20:02,540 --> 00:20:02,920
and peace.

463
00:20:03,440 --> 00:20:04,960
You don't have to be available to everyone.

464
00:20:05,460 --> 00:20:07,300
You don't have to explain every choice.

465
00:20:07,900 --> 00:20:09,840
And you don't have to fear an empty room.

466
00:20:09,840 --> 00:20:13,480
Sometimes being alone gives you the space to hear yourself clearly.

467
00:20:14,120 --> 00:20:17,700
But remember, the purpose of understanding yourself isn't to disconnect from the

468
00:20:17,700 --> 00:20:17,960
world.

469
00:20:18,520 --> 00:20:21,260
It's to understand how you want to participate in it.

470
00:20:21,660 --> 00:20:22,120
Think deeper.

471
00:20:22,660 --> 00:20:23,120
Live better.

472
00:20:23,620 --> 00:20:24,140
Become more.

473
00:20:24,640 --> 00:20:26,060
This is why we become.

